/**
 * The one operation a bf16 value still has no overload for is `%`, and not because of the
 * precision: `%` is refused on every floating type, f32 included (see
 * test/cases/compile/operators/float_mod_assign.cml), so `__mod__` is registered for the integer
 * types only.
 *
 * The refusal is the ordinary one: no matching function for `(bf16, bf16)`, "operator '%' is not
 * defined for these operand types" -- the same sentence the call gets over f32. bf16 and f16 are
 * floating types of the language (design principles 7.3), so nothing about the element width is a
 * reason to report the call differently: the operation is missing for every float, and the message
 * says that. The refusal is part of compiling the program, so the empty array is never read at
 * run time.
 *
 * The arithmetic and comparison a bf16 value does have -- `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `^`, `==`, `<`, ...
 * (design principles 7.3) -- are not refused, and
 * test/cases/modules/stdlib/reduced_scalar.cml pins what they compute. This case used to assert
 * the refusal of `+`, which was true before then; it is re-pointed at the operation that is
 * genuinely refused rather than deleted.
 */

func main(): int sync {
    let xs: bf16[] = []
    println(xs[0] % xs[0])
    return 0
}